 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S442-021\",\n \"clauses\": {\"compare\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F05\", \"template\": \"Which has a greater value: {desc1} or {desc2}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 is the question template for comparing two money piles and states the comparison task directly. Its desc slots draw from F07, F09 and F11 with distinct_in_every_render true. The F07 nesting is faulty (see failing_families), but F05 itself produces comparison problems.\"},\n  \"different_denominations\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F05\", \"template\": \"Which has a greater value: {desc1} or {desc2}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 with desc slots drawn from F09 printed pairs gives two piles of different denominations in one problem, as the distinct_in_every_render true value shows. Each printed F09 pair (1 P1 coin, 1 P10 coin, 1 P100 bill, 1 P20 bill, 1 P5 coin, 1 P50 bill) is a distinct denomination.\"},\n  \"peso_coins\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F09\", \"template\": \"{answer}\", \"reasoning\": \"F09 printed values include the P1, P5 and P10 coin pairs, which are peso coins. The printed list is observed and includes these coin values.\"},\n  \"excluding_centavo_coins\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F09\", \"template\": \"{answer}\", \"reasoning\": \"No centavo value appears in F08 members or in F09 printed values, and F09 prints only peso denominations. Problems built from F09 therefore show peso coins and bills only, which addresses the exclusion.\"},\n  \"bills\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F09\", \"template\": \"{answer}\", \"reasoning\": \"F09 printed values include the P20, P50 and P100 bill pairs, so problems built from F09 show bills as the clause requires.\"},\n  \"up_to_100\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F09\", \"template\": \"{answer}\", \"reasoning\": \"F09 printed values reach 100 as the largest denomination and F08 members are at most 100, which matches the declared max_total of 100. Values stay within the range of the clause.\"}},\n \"failing_families\": [\"F07\"],\n \"failing_templates\": [\"{count} {face} {unit}\"],\n \"overall\": \"FAIL\",\n \"reasoning\": \"F07 template '{count} {face} {unit}' has its unit slot drawn from the generator's own choices (bill, bills, coin, coins) with no link to the face slot or to count. The face slot draws from F08 members 1, 5, 10, 20, 50 and 100. Because the unit is not tied to the denomination, F07 can print a coin for a denomination the curriculum has only as a bill (the 20, 50 and 100 faces), which is factually wrong for a grade 1 learner of peso coins and bills. Because count is fixed at 1 (lo=1, hi=1) while unit can be plural, F07 can also print a plural unit with a count of one, which is ungrammatical for grade 1. F07 is nested in F05 and F06 and appears in correct_answer, distractors and format_data options, so these defects reach the keyed answer and the choices as well. F05 and F06 are not listed as failing families because their own templates are sound; the fault is the F07 slot domain. F09 printed values are correctly paired and observed, so F09 passes. The competency wording, peso-only denominations and the cumulative vocabulary (no NOT_YET_KNOWN term appears in any template) are otherwise within scope. The word greater is not in cumulative_vocab, but it is also not in NOT_YET_KNOWN, so it was not counted as a gate violation.\"}",
